From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Extroponeurship: Possible idea?, and Schelling Points...

Phil <pgf@srl03.cacs.usl.edu> sez-

> I think it's reading the program from the hard disk. Into Ram.
> Filling it up. Then writing program back out to hard disk virtual
> memory...
> ...
> I thought that (_if_ the controller could multitask or something like 
> that) that having the swap space and programs on two different drives would
> make execution and the use of virtual memory much "cleaner." Any comments
> on that, and the "dual disk" thing in general?

A good operating system (I think Mach does this) will map your program to
read-only address space straight from the program file.  Mach even has a
thing where if you try to write to a page in a mapped-in piece of disk, it
interrupts, copies the page of RAM, maps you to the copy, and then
returns from the interrupt, letting you modify your own copy of that one
page, but giving the virtuality of a whole writable copy of the file.

Otherwise, dual disks... in principal you could have two accesses going on
at once and increase your throughput.  You could also do mirroring and
things like that (mirror only changed stuff until backup...)--get some
of the features of RAIDs.

