For _some_ investments you can, such as mutual funds and other managed
investment strategies. But it is nearly impossible to acquire statistics
on the %ROI and %STDEV on individual investment strategies. This can be
for a number of reasons. Some people, such as yourself, are not willing
do publicly divulge specifics. More often, in all likelihood, those figures
divulged are inaccurate or biased in some way.

Certainly, if any given person _has_ a history of their investments, they
can acquire these figures and then plot them against other, known investments
such as mutual funds and the like. However, I believe we're talking more in
the context of the new investor who wishes to compare different strategies.
Beyond hearsay, there is little data. We are told that stockbrokers are
no good (and I know there are some studies in this area). Some say
contrarians do well, others say they do no better. Other such as yourself
claim that people of some intelligence and common sense can do well. How
well? So you see, how do you plot these things on the graph? _These_ are
the statistics I am talking about.

Now, I'm not claiming they don't exist - I'm simply asking where they are.
My original comments about the government probably being able to provide
better insight here relates to a number of things. For one, the brokerage
firms would _certainly_ never provide statistics which show that they don't
provide any value to the average stock market investor (I'm not talking
about mutual funds here). Anyone on Wall Street is going to push whatever
it is they do. So, I am still asking, are there reports from _relatively_
unbiased govt. agencies, schools, and the like which give some statistics
on this?

Tony Hamilton
