From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: axioms 

D. Anton Sherwood says:
> > The sort of thing I have in mind is Mises' action axiom:
> > human action is purposeful behavior.  Attempting to deny it would
> > constitute means (an argument) to an end (a conclusion), that is, it would
> > be purposeful behavior, human action.
> 
> Bzzt!  The negation of Mises is "not all human action is purposeful",
> and is not necessarily contradicted by an attempt at proof.

One should also keep in mind that Mises analysis works perfectly well
if you start with the assumption "MOST human action is puposeful TO
THE HUMAN." One need not assume an absolute for his reasoning to
retain value.

One should not fall into the trap of believing that a one to one
mapping exists between formal systems and the English language.
Unfortunately, the real world contains too many ambiguities to use
absolutes -- especially ones phrased in a pesky language like English.

For instance, the state OCCASSIONALLY does some good. My problem with
it is that OVERALL it is a bad thing. I would not claim that NO ONE is
ever helped by the state.

There are also times when initiating force against others makes sense
-- for instance, lets say that someone is standing on their property
and is personally blocking the only path of escape you have from a
fire. You'd be an idiot not to knock them over and deal with the
consequences later.

In general, absolutes do not lead to strong reasoning outside of areas
that may be translated into formal systems. Computer programs and
mathematics are fine spots for assuming absolutes in your reasoning --
but deciding how to behave in an amgiguous situation is not always as
cut and dried.

